              {
                "id": "R1-001",
                "lens": "risk",
                "location": "engine/review/fsstore/portable.go:24-36",
                "severity": "WARNING",
                "claim": "On non-linux platforms Lock holds no real lock, so the one-correction-round rule can still be bypassed by two concurrent blocking verify calls on two heads of one base. The limitation is documented in the code, README and CHANGELOG, and the race existed at baseline, so it is not a regression.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/review/fsstore/portable.go:24-36",
                  "odd/review-results/README.md:81"
                ],
                "causal_disposition": "pre-existing"
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Linux Lock in engine/review/fsstore/held_linux.go opens the feature directory through openHeld (no symlink followed, nothing created), takes an exclusive flock retried on EINTR, and releases it by closing the descriptor. On error the descriptor is closed, so there is no descriptor leak.",
              "Verify takes the lock only for a blocking verdict, before the stored verdicts are listed, and defers the unlock until after the verdict is stored. This closes the check-then-store race and adds no new path-traversal or symlink exposure, since Lock goes through the same walk as Names.",
              "The feature name is validated by review.CheckFeature before Lock is reached, and the new test covers an unsafe name, a symlinked directory and an absent directory.",
              "No secrets, new dependencies or unsafe input handling are introduced. The remaining items are docs and tests."
            ]
          }
        }
