            "lens": "review-resilience",
            "findings": [
              {
                "id": "R4-lock-unbounded-wait",
                "lens": "resilience",
                "location": "engine/review/fsstore/held_linux.go:48-53",
                "severity": "WARNING",
                "claim": "Lock blocks in flock(LOCK_EX) with no timeout or cancellation. If a holder process hangs between taking the lock and storing the verdict (for example a stalled filesystem), every other blocking verify on that feature waits indefinitely with no diagnostic. The lock is released on process exit, so a crash is safe, but a hang is not. The window is small because the held section only lists names and creates one file, so this is a robustness gap rather than a demonstrated failure.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/review/fsstore/held_linux.go:48-53",
                  "engine/review/app/verify.go:86-90"
                ],
                "evidence_class": "inferential",
                "causal_disposition": "introduced"
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Reviewed all 10 changed paths. The lock is taken only for blocking verdicts, released via defer on every return path in Verify, and acquired before the stored verdicts are listed, which closes the race the change targets. EINTR is retried, the directory is closed on flock failure, and the unlock closes the descriptor. A missing directory returns ErrNotFound without creating anything, and a symlink is refused through openHeld.",
              "The portable fallback holds no lock and documents it in the code comment, README and CHANGELOG, so the degradation is disclosed rather than silent.",
              "The tests cover lock ordering (Lock, Names, Create, Unlock), a failed lock storing nothing, mutual exclusion on linux, and refusal of absent, linked and unsafe feature directories."
            ]
          }
        }
